// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Baked default men's pants mesh (replaces the procedural pants surface).
// Source: cbotm_casbagy_pants.obj — Saints Row map editor export
// (cmesh_xbox2), 380 vertices / 516 triangles, no UVs/normals/materials in
// the source file (normals computed here), single static mesh covering
// both legs from waist to ankle in a relaxed standing pose — no
// skeleton/bone weights, so it cannot bend at the knee like the existing
// per-joint procedural leg parts.
//
// Vertex coordinates are kept in their native, un-recentered values: the
// OBJ's authored Y range (~0 at the ankle to ~0.965 at the waist) already
// lines up almost exactly with this rig's `body`-local space at rest (hip
// joints sit at y=0.96, shoes bottom out around y=0.04), so the mesh is
// attached directly to `body` (not to a per-leg hip/knee joint) and
// rendered as a static outer layer. It moves rigidly with the torso/hips
// during crouch and overall body bob, but does not flex at the knee during
// walk/run — the procedural leg cylinders underneath are kept (just
// slimmed down) as joint fillers so no gap shows through at the knee when
// it bends away from the resting pose.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ─ Pants split into 3 pieces so each leg follows its hip joint ─────────────
// Waist piece stays on `body`; legR/legL are in hip-local coords and get
// parented to legR.hip / legL.hip so they swing with the leg exactly like
// the shoes do. Vertices were split by face centroid x-position in Python
// (waist = y>0.80; right = centroid x>0; left = centroid x<0) and each
